    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d719">

                <pb facs="0851" n="755" sameAs="frus:frus1921v02/pg755"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">860n.01/32</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Russian Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bakhmeteff</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Assistant Secretary of
                        State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Dearing</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-07-01"><hi rendition="#italic">July 1,
                        1921</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Dearing</hi>: Referring to our recent
                    conversation I take pleasure in forwarding herewith an <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Aide Memoire</hi> on the problem of the Baltic States. The point of view
                    expressed therein is typical of Russian constructive opinion. The basic
                    conceptions <pb facs="0852" n="756" xml:id="pg756"/> found expression as early
                    as March, 1919, in a communication addressed to the President of the Peace
                    Conference by the Russian Delegation in Paris which unofficially represented
                    national opinion during the negotiations of peace.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">B. Bakhmeteff</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d719-a1.xml" xpointer="d719-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
